---
import Calculator from "../components/Calculator.astro";
import PointsTables from "../components/PointsTables.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { getPolicyView } from "../lib/policy.js";

const policy = getPolicyView();
const tierGroups = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((tier) => ({
  tier,
  tournaments: policy.tournaments.filter((tournament) => tournament.tier === tier),
}));
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="The exact National Points Race scoring policy, calculation rules, sources, and correction process."
>
  <header class="shell hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Policy {policy.version}</p>
    <h1>How the race is calculated</h1>
    <p class="dek">
      These tables are rendered from the same versioned policy ledger used by the
      scoring engine. No separate editorial spreadsheet controls the result.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Tier 2 through Tier 5</p><h2>Placement and elimination points</h2></div></div>
      <PointsTables {policy} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Tier 1</p><h2>NSDA Nationals</h2></div>
        <p>International and United States Extemp are scored separately, then one division may receive the strong-field multiplier.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-grid">
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Place</th><th class="numeric" scope="col">Base points</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{policy.nsda.basePlacements.map((points, index) => <tr><td>{index + 1}</td><td class="numeric"><strong>{points}</strong></td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="paper-card prose">
          <h3>NSDA adjustments</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>The division containing more competitors from the post-NCFL top 25 receives a 1.25 multiplier.</li>
            <li>Multiplication uses half-up rounding to the nearest whole point.</li>
            <li>The final-round winner receives a 40-point base bonus. It is also multiplied in the strong-field division.</li>
            <li>Quarterfinalists receive 30 base points. Octafinalists receive 10.</li>
            <li>If both divisions contain the same number of top-25 competitors, neither receives the multiplier.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell"><Calculator {policy} /></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Current roster</p><h2>Twenty-one tracked tournaments</h2></div><p>Names and tiers come directly from the executable 2026-27 policy.</p></div>
      <p class="asu-note">
        The <strong>Arizona State HDSHC Invitational</strong> joins the race as a
        <strong>Tier 4</strong> tournament beginning in 2026-27. It is not applied
        retroactively to the 2025-26 reconstruction.
      </p>
      <div class="tier-roster">
        {tierGroups.map(({ tier, tournaments }) => (
          <section class="tier-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">Tier {tier}</p>
            <ul class="clean-list">
              {tournaments.map((tournament) => <li><strong>{tournament.canonicalName}</strong>{tournament.mbaTopSixOnly && <span>MBA top-six exception</span>}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split-grid">
      <article class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Finals and divisions</p>
        <h2>Edge cases are policy, too.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Places 1 through 6 receive placement points in a standard final.</li>
          <li>In an oversized final, seventh place or lower falls to the preceding elimination bucket when the tier has one.</li>
          <li>MBA awards points only to its top six. Its legacy final-credit limit is five for the finals tiebreak.</li>
          <li>When a competitor enters multiple eligible divisions at one tournament, only the highest single award counts.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Standings and evidence</p>
        <h2>Sorting, corrections, and restraint.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Standings sort by points, wins, top-three finishes, then finals. Exact competitive ties share a rank.</li>
          <li>Official corrections produce a new standings version and preserve prior history.</li>
          <li>Ambiguous identities and contradictory sources are withheld. The system does not guess.</li>
          <li>Unavailable or not-held tournaments remain visible with that status and award no points.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .asu-note { max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 2rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .tier-roster { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 2.5rem; }
  .tier-roster section:first-child { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  .tier-section { border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-dark); padding-top: 1rem; }
  .tier-roster li { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; }
  .tier-roster li span { color: var(--burgundy); font-size: 0.75rem; }
  @media (max-width: 44rem) { .tier-roster { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .tier-roster section:first-child { grid-column: auto; } }
</style>
